  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A zero-cost collar occurs when the premium received from selling one option (e.g., writing a floor) exactly offsets the premium paid for buying the other option (e.g., purchasing a cap). The net premium cost is zero, but the entity accepts limited upside in exchange for downside protection. ASC 815 governs derivative accounting.
Wrong (A): Setting both strikes at the current market rate does not define a zero-cost collar; it is the premium offset that makes the collar zero-cost.
Wrong (C): All derivative instruments must have a notional amount under ASC 815; lacking one would disqualify the instrument as a derivative.
Wrong (D): Perfect hedge effectiveness is a separate concept from zero cost; a collar can be zero-cost without achieving 100% effectiveness.$EXPL$
